$WW,1$$FG,14$$TX+CX,"ThinkPad T4xx"$$FG$

$TX+CX,"The embedded controller demos in this folder, and what else"$
$TX+CX,"of a T420, T430 or T440p works in TinkerOS."$

$FG,10$The EC demos in this folder$FG$

$TX+CX,"Tried on a T420 and a T430.  Nothing else."$

These talk to the Lenovo ThinkPad embedded controller through the
ACPI EC ports: the battery pack's own registers, the fan tachometer
and level, and the thermal sensors.

$FG,4$The EC offsets used here are the ones Lenovo's firmware uses on
the T420 and the T430, and those are the only two machines any of
this has been run on.$FG$  A different ThinkPad, another vendor's
laptop, or a desktop will read whatever happens to sit at those
offsets, so expect nonsense rather than numbers.  Reading the EC is
harmless anywhere; writing to it at a guessed offset is not, which
is why the fan control paths all go through one gate you can shut
off (see the end of this file).

$FG,10$Files$FG$

  EC.HC       ACPI table search, EC ports out of the ECDT, and the
              locked read/write primitives.  The others include it.
  Battery.HC  Smart Battery state through the EC's page window.
  Fan.HC      Fan RPM, level, thermal sensors, fan control.
  FanGui.HC   A fan fader with live temperatures.

$FG,10$Usage$FG$

  Include the one you want; it pulls in what it needs.

$FG,2$Battery$FG$

  #include "::/Demo/Lenovo/T4xx/Battery"

  BatRep;          Charge, rate, voltage, status, design figures and
                   the pack's name, for both slots.
  BatRep(TRUE);    The same, plus the ACPI tables behind it.
  BatPct;          BAT0 percent, -1 if there is no answer.
  BatWatch;        BAT0 once a second.  Any key stops it.
  BatTblRep;       Which ACPI tables declare battery devices.

$FG,2$Fan and temperatures$FG$

  #include "::/Demo/Lenovo/T4xx/Fan"

  FanRep;          RPM, level and every live sensor.  The everyday one.
  FanWatch;        The same, live.  Any key stops it.
  TempRep;         Sensors only.  Sensor 0 is the CPU.
  FanSetLevel(4);  Manual level 0-7.  Level 0 stops the fan, and what
                   happens next is then your problem.
  FanSetFullSpeed; Off the governor, all the way up.  Loud, never unsafe.
  FanSetAuto;      Hand the fan back to the firmware.  Always safe.
  FanCurve;        Software governor on the hottest sensor.  Any key
                   stops it, and it returns the fan to auto by itself
                   if a sensor disappears, a write fails, or anything
                   crosses the panic temperature.

$FG,2$The GUI$FG$

  #include "::/Demo/Lenovo/T4xx/FanGui"

  FanTempGui;      Fan fader plus live temperatures and a bar per
                   sensor.  Drag the fader to take manual control,
                   $FG,11$A$FG$ for auto, $FG,11$F$FG$ for full speed, $FG,11$ESC$FG$ to close.  It
                   backs off to auto on its own if it gets hot.

$FG,2$Poking at the EC by hand$FG$

  When the numbers above look wrong for your machine, this is where
  you start:

  #include "::/Demo/Lenovo/T4xx/EC"

  EcDump;          Hex dump of the 256 byte EC address space.
  EcSnap;          Remember it, then change something (unplug the
  EcDiff;          charger, let the fan spin up) and see what moved.
  EcPctCandidates; Offsets holding a plausible percentage.

$FG,10$Turning the writes off$FG$

  Every path that writes to the EC goes through FanCtrlOk, gated on
  FAN_ALLOW_CONTROL in Fan.HC.  It ships as 1.  Set it to 0 and the
  fan calls refuse, the reports still work, and FanTempGui comes up
  display only with a red line saying so.  Do that first on any
  machine that is not a T420 or a T430.

$FG,14$$TX+CX,"Other T4xx hardware"$$FG$

  These live elsewhere in the tree and ship with the distro; they
  are listed here because this is where the T4xx notes are.

$FG,10$Webcam -- T430 and T440p$FG$

  The built-in camera works on the T430 (Chicony) and the T440p
  (Bison), over the UVC driver.  Pictures save as .GR files.

  #include "::/Apps/WebCam/WebCam"

  WebCam;              320x240 live window.  Space takes a picture,
                       S or Enter saves it to ~/Pictures/CamNNN.GR,
                       ESC quits.
  WebCam(640,480);     VGA.  WebCam(1280,720); is 720p over MJPEG.
  CamStream;           Half size, lower right corner, stays on top
                       and never takes the keyboard.  CamStreamStop;
                       closes it.
  CamSnap;             One 640x480 picture, no window, saved and the
                       name returned.
  CamRep;              The camera's formats, sizes and frame rates.
  CamDiag;             Stream for three seconds and report what
                       arrived.  Start here when nothing shows up.

  ::/Apps/WebCam/Help.DD has the rest.

$FG,10$Ethernet -- T420, T430 and T440p$FG$

  The built-in wired NIC works on all three: the 82579LM of the T420
  and T430, and the I217-LM of the T440p, both on the E1000E driver.
  Wireless is not supported on any of them.

  #include "::/Demo/Net/Start"

  That finds the card, loads its driver and runs NetCfg for a DHCP
  lease; it says whether it got one and prints NetRep.  Afterwards:

  NetRep;              The UDP, TCP, DNS, ARP and IPv4 state,
                       the address it was given included.
  NetCfg;              Ask for a lease again.
  Ping("1.1.1.1");     Or a hostname, which also exercises DNS.

  Then the programs in /Demo/Net/Programs (FTP, telnet, gopher,
  chat).  Plug the cable in before you include Start, and if
  something drops to Dbg, G2; carries on.

$FG,10$USB -- T420, T430 and T440p$FG$

  All three machines' EHCI ports work, and so do the xHCI ports of
  the T440p: keyboards, mice, serial adapters, webcams, and sticks,
  which mount by themselves when you plug them in (the top bar says
  +USB DRV and its drive letter).

  UsbRep;              Host controllers and everything attached,
                       with the kind of each device.
